wnloaded update force-installs. Only applies when auto-updates are enabled. Blank = 72-hour default.",id:"VyqfjIW6Iv"})),long:K.longDescription("Has no effect when `disableAutoUpdates` is in place at launch: the updater never starts, so nothing is downloaded and this timer never arms. If the policy reaches an already-running app after an update has downloaded, that one staged update still installs on this timer; no further updates are fetched.\n\nLeaving it blank uses the 72-hour default *and* then waits for the machine to be idle (10+ minutes without input) before restarting; setting any explicit value (including 72) restarts once the window elapses regardless of user activity. In both cases the restart holds off while Claude is mid-task.")},predicates:{show:(e)=>{var t;return((t=e.autoUpdate)===null||t===void 0?void 0:t.disabled)!==!0}}}),viaUpdatesHost:K.field(De.z.boolean().optional(),{flatKey:"updateViaUpdatesHost",remotePolicy:{hotApplied:!0},support:{enabled:{scopes:["3p"],availableInVersion:"1.26832.0"}},appBehaviorOnly:!0,title:B.msg({defaultMessage:"Check for updates on releases.claude.com",id:"UpdateViaUpdatesHost"}),default:!1,category:"telemetry",group:xr.UPDATES_GROUP,order:22,description:{short:K.shortDescription(B.msg({defaultMessage:"Read the update feed from releases.claude.com so api.anthropic.com can stay blocked.",id:"UpdateViaUpdatesHostShort"})),long:K.longDescription("By default the app asks `api.anthropic.com` which version to install. That host also serves the model APIs, so organizations that block un-approved LLM endpoints at the network edge end up blocking updates too.\n\nTurn this on to read the same feed from `releases.claude.com`, a hostname that carries no model API. `api.anthropic.com` can then stay blocked without breaking auto-update. Rollout behavior is unchanged; the installer download still comes from `downloads.claude.ai` as before.")},predicates:{show:(e)=>{var t;return((t=e.autoUpdate)===null||t===void 0?void 0:t.disabled)!==!0}}}),dangerousMaxVersion:K.field(zn.desktopVersionString().optional(),{flatKey:"dangerousMaxVersion",support:{enabled:{scopes:["3p"],availableInVersion:"2.110.0"},visibility:"public-undocumented"},appBehaviorOnly:!0,title:B.msg({defaultMessage:"Maximum version",id:"DangerousMaxVersion"}),category:"telemetry",group:xr.UPDATES_GROUP,order:22.5,description:{short:K.shortDescription(B.msg({defaultMessage:"Third-party deployments only. Auto-update stops at this version (X.Y.Z) and devices on newer versions cannot open Claude. Delays security fixes; leave unset.",id:"DangerousMaxVersionShort"})),long:K.longDescription("Third-party deployments only. Caps the Claude version your devices run. Update checks take the newest release at or below this version and nothing newer. Devices on a version from before this setting existed don't send the cap, so they update past it and are then blocked: move your fleet onto a version that supports this setting, or turn off auto-updates, before you set a cap below the latest release. Use `X.Y.Z` form, e.g. `1.2581.0`; `v1.2581.0` also works.\n\nA device on a newer version, including a fresh install of one, cannot open Claude. It shows a message that names both versions and asks the person to contact their admin, and the only option is to quit. Only a cap your organization delivers blocks: a device-management profile or machine policy, the admin console, or a bootstrap server. A cap set only in the local Setup UI, or in a per-user Windows policy, caps updates but does not block. When a profile that configures the connection also names a configuration server, the served configuration is authoritative once fetched and must restate the cap; the profile's value blocks only while the server can't be reached, and before sign-in it caps updates without blocking. A profile that sets only app-behavior keys wins over a served value. The cap never downgrades or rolls back a device. An update that finished downloading before the cap reached a device can still install, and that device is then blocked too.\n\nWhile it is set, security and compatibility fixes in newer releases do not install, so Anthropic recommends leaving it unset. If you use it, raise it as you approve each new version: devices below the new value update to it on their next update check, and `autoUpdaterEnforcementHours` still restarts them within its window.\n\n`disableAutoUpdates` takes precedence for updates: with both set, nothing updates, and devices on a newer version are still blocked. On Linux, where the package manager (apt) delivers updates, it caps nothing but still blocks newer versions.")},predicates:{warn:(e)=>e?B.msg({defaultMessage:"Devices on a newer version cannot open Claude, and security fixes in newer releases do not install, until you raise or remove this cap.",id:"DangerousMaxVersionWarn"}):null}})});var Gue=B.msg({defaultMessage:"Feature announcements",id:"FeatureAnnouncementsGroup"});lj.FeatureDiscoveryConfig=De.z.object({disabled:K.field(De.z.boolean().optional(),{flatKey:"disableFeatureDiscovery",remotePolicy:{hotApplied:!0},support:{enabled:{scopes:["3p"],availableInVersion:"1.21459.0"}},title:B.msg({defaultMessage:"Hide feature announcements",id:"DisableFeatureDiscoveryTitle"}),default:!1,failClosedValue:!0,category:"appearance",group:Gue,order:10,description:{short:K.shortDescription(B.msg({defaultMessage:"Suppress unprompted feature-announcement UI: the post-update \u201CWhat\u2019s new\u201D nudge and new-feature tips. Users can still open release notes themselves.",id:"DisableFeatureDiscoveryHint"})),long:K.longDescription(`Covers the version-shipped announcement UI baked into each release: the **What's new** button that appears on its own after an update, and the one-time **New feature** tips (coach-marks) that point out newly shipped capabilities. Useful when your organization gates feature availability and doesn't want the app advertising capabilities you haven't rolled out.

